WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:02.010
Ugh, would you calm down?

00:00:02.010 --> 00:00:03.190
Now read my lips.

00:00:03.190 --> 00:00:04.350
We gotta talk.

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:06.930
Narrator: One of the hardest
jobs in visual effects

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:10.420
is making animals look
realistic when they talk.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:14.220
It's a challenge effects studio
Weta Digital has taken on.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.020
And if you look at Weta's talking fox

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.620
from the 2021 movie "The Green Knight,"

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:21.000
you can see just how far we've come.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.496
But getting from here ...

00:00:22.496 --> 00:00:24.550
Don: [neighs] Oh, my. Can't ask him that.

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:25.383
Narrator: To here ...

00:00:25.383 --> 00:00:28.444
Fox: To end his song as he saw fit.

00:00:28.444 --> 00:00:30.660
Narrator: Wasn't easy.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:34.540
When the whole CG-talking-animal
genre was born in the '90s,

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:37.640
filmmakers faced one
overarching challenge:

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:39.423
how do you make a fundamentally

00:00:39.423 --> 00:00:41.872
unbelievable thing believable?

00:00:41.872 --> 00:00:44.620
Before, movies would just
dub over the mouth movements

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:47.480
of live animal actors
or animatronic puppets

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.190
and later moved to compositing
footage of live animals

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:53.040
with 3D digital models
of the animals' mouths.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.900
Over the years, studios like
Weta have mastered the art

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.510
of creating fully digital
creatures that speak

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:01.650
and express themselves
in naturalistic ways.

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:03.370
Mike was one of the lead animators

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:05.150
on "Rise of the Planet of the Apes,"

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:07.430
where the chimpanzee Caesar slowly learns

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:09.466
to converse with humans.

00:01:09.466 --> 00:01:10.930
Caesar: No!

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:12.570
Narrator: Human actors not only voiced

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:14.100
but actually played the apes,

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.720
filming in motion-capture suits

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.503
that tracked their movements.

00:01:17.503 --> 00:01:20.180
Weta's animators then had
to map those performances

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:22.070
onto the digital ape models,

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:23.820
making the apes deliver their lines

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:26.878
and emote in ways that stayed
true to the actors' choices

00:01:26.878 --> 00:01:29.290
without feeling too human.

00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:31.470
The very different
anatomies of humans and apes

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:32.830
often made it impossible

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:35.170
to exactly translate those acting choices

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:37.250
one-for-one in dialogue scenes.

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:39.290
For example, apes have heavier brows

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:40.957
and larger neck muscles,

00:01:40.957 --> 00:01:43.340
which had to move differently
when the apes were speaking.

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:45.690
Making sure that we've got
the wrinkling working well,

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:46.910
how the muzzle inflates,

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:49.570
how still he is when he says his line,

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:52.300
sometimes that isn't a dead translation.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:54.340
Narrator: Nowhere are the
anatomical differences

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:56.550
more extreme than in that mouth area,

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:59.245
which is obviously crucial for dialogue.

00:01:59.245 --> 00:02:01.380
The muzzle on a chimpanzee, for example,

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:04.600
is a lot more malleable and
dexterous than a human mouth.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.350
The apes have the ability
to move their lips

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:09.080
in such big ways compared to humans.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:10.562
Narrator: That meant that in some cases,

00:02:10.562 --> 00:02:13.770
animators had to blend some of
the actors' human lip shapes

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:16.350
onto the digital facial
puppets of the apes,

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:18.570
like when pronouncing the phoneme "sh."

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:22.340
A chimp's lips extend into a
long pucker to make this shape,

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:24.690
which would distract
from the actual dialogue.

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:27.310
So Weta reined that in,
tightening the mouth shape

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:29.820
and keeping a fuller, more human lip.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:31.300
These lip and snout differences

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:33.960
between humans and apes
actually made Andy Serkis

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:36.310
a perfect casting choice for Caesar,

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:38.220
since he often articulates with his lips

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:39.840
while delivering lines.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.480
Andy can be an overperformer.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:43.970
He started in early
days of motion capture,

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:46.480
when the fidelity wasn't as good.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:47.890
It didn't capture as much motion,

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:51.050
so when you see him perform for Caesar,

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:52.720
he does move his lips a little bit more

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:54.323
than you typically expect

00:02:54.323 --> 00:02:56.430
when you actually talk to him in person.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:58.530
Narrator: Beyond the most
visible parts of the mouth,

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:00.030
Weta spends a lot of time looking

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:02.220
at dental scans of actors' teeth

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:05.690
and videos showing how the
tongue moves inside the mouth.

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:08.820
All that internal
structure inevitably drives

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:10.995
how the surface of the mouth behaves,

00:03:10.995 --> 00:03:12.628
how the body behaves.

00:03:12.628 --> 00:03:15.900
Apes and any other character
or animal we're doing,

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:18.530
we can actually take the
geometry of their mouth,

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:21.680
their teeth, their gums, their tongue,

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:23.330
so that the same way we know

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:26.370
how lips move over a human's skeleton,

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:29.845
the same thing's going to
happen with a dog, with an ape.

00:03:29.845 --> 00:03:32.012
Narrator: That close
understanding of mouth anatomy

00:03:32.012 --> 00:03:33.890
came into play on "The Jungle Book,"

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:36.678
where Weta had to make the
giant orangutan King Louie

00:03:36.678 --> 00:03:38.760
not only talk, but sing.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:40.860
Animators had to figure out just how far

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:42.770
to hold Louie's tongue back in his mouth

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:44.325
when he sang his lines.

00:03:44.325 --> 00:03:46.624
♪ You'll see it's true ♪

00:03:46.624 --> 00:03:49.370
♪ Someone like me ♪

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:52.083
In the end, though, animation
supervisor Paul Story

00:03:52.083 --> 00:03:54.330
said his team had to sell
the singing performance

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:56.290
with body motion first,

00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:58.880
like by carefully timing
Louie's chest movements

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:01.610
to show him breathing
in between lines of song

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:03.310
and by coordinating his body movements

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:04.990
with every little head motion,

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:06.690
a lot of which was based on videos

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:09.520
of Christopher Walken reading his lines.

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:11.330
When Weta made dogs talk and sing

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:14.090
for another live-action
remake, "Lady and the Tramp,"

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:17.492
animators kept an even lighter
touch on the mouth area.

00:04:17.492 --> 00:04:20.560
The difference between ape's
mouth and a dog's mouth is,

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.200
you know, a dog's got a long muzzle,

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:27.000
and as soon as you start
doing too much with the jaw,

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.890
it suddenly becomes this
overanimated performance,

00:04:29.890 --> 00:04:31.640
and if you've got really fast dialogue

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:34.720
or somebody singing a song,
it quickly falls apart.

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:36.818
The thing that we found with dogs

00:04:36.818 --> 00:04:39.320
and the fox in particular was

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:41.970
if we pushed the performance
out to the front of the mouth

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:45.460
and then supported that
performance in the rest of the face

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:47.120
and all the way down into the body,

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:49.590
I think that really kind of grounds it.

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:51.950
Lady: Well, this is goodbye, I think.

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:52.783
Tramp: Yep.

00:04:52.783 --> 00:04:53.970
Lady: Bye.
Tramp: Bye.

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:56.250
Narrator: This approach to
the fox in "The Green Knight"

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:58.300
helped guard the mystery around him.

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:01.060
Since he talks in what
feels like a dream state,

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:03.920
director David Lowery wanted
to keep viewers guessing.

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:06.830
Fox: Are you this man?

00:05:06.830 --> 00:05:10.060
Mike: The dialogue was almost like,

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:12.290
wait, did I just see that?

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:13.380
Is that talking?

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:15.210
David wanted to keep it so subtle

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:18.660
and such a character that
you never really understood.

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:20.020
Narrator: So the animators focused

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:21.498
on making the fox's breath

00:05:21.498 --> 00:05:23.980
move through his body as he speaks

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:25.640
and on nailing the subtle movements

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:27.990
in the nose, neck, and jowls.

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:29.620
They even zoomed into parts of the body

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:32.230
you wouldn't think of
as being tied to speech,

00:05:32.230 --> 00:05:34.150
like timing the fox's vocal delivery

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:37.120
with his footsteps and
the twitching of his ears.

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:40.160
They're all folding into
that little bit of dialogue,

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:43.120
so the dialogue may be subtle
on the front of the muzzle,

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:46.010
but the whole character is speaking.

00:05:46.010 --> 00:05:48.410
Narrator: Including,
maybe most importantly,

00:05:48.410 --> 00:05:49.447
through the eyes.

00:05:49.447 --> 00:05:52.233
We've always known that
the eyes are where you get

00:05:52.233 --> 00:05:54.439
a lot of the performance
out of the characters.

00:05:54.439 --> 00:05:56.210
Narrator: That's why Weta's
always improving the way

00:05:56.210 --> 00:05:58.310
it models its eyes, including parts

00:05:58.310 --> 00:06:00.300
that we don't even see on camera.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:02.910
Eric: If you go back to
Gollum, the eyes we had,

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:05.460
it was three pieces of geometry

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:06.890
with painted textures on them.

00:06:06.890 --> 00:06:08.700
Our eyes have so much depth in them now

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:10.610
that you can actually fly right into them.

00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:13.900
We actually put all of the
proper strings and muscles in,

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:16.710
connecting the pupil and
generating the iris, everything.

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:18.120
We've talked to eye doctors.

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:19.557
We've got eye scans.

00:06:19.557 --> 00:06:21.090
Narrator: Weta's animators have found

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:25.250
that for dialogue scenes,
nailing eye focus is key.

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:27.840
And so we're really
specific about making sure

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:30.470
that there's something,
even in the digital space,

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:33.010
the off-camera, that isn't
even going to be in the shot

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:34.970
that the characters are focusing on.

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:36.180
Narrator: The studio's also learned

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:38.680
that our eyes are constantly
making tiny movements

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:40.520
to take in new pieces of a scene,

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:43.370
even when we appear to
be holding a steady gaze.

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:45.770
There's this extra little bit of motion

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:49.060
that sits on top of all
that detailed performance.

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:51.380
Narrator: For emotional
dialogue moments like this one,

00:06:51.380 --> 00:06:53.170
the team consulted with an eye surgeon

00:06:53.170 --> 00:06:55.530
to learn how moisture
beads up on the eyeballs

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:56.940
and runs into the fold

00:06:56.940 --> 00:06:58.970
between the lower lid and the eyeball.

00:06:58.970 --> 00:07:00.980
The blood flow as someone's crying,

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:03.320
you get that actual
reddening in the caruncula.

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:05.560
You really feel that emotion come through

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:06.820
with all that extra detail.

00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:08.580
Narrator: That obsessive level of detail

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:10.160
has made it possible to branch out

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:12.050
into new types of talking animals,

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:13.150
even the swimming kind,

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:15.540
in season two of "The Umbrella Academy."

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:18.170
After creating the chimp
Pogo for the Netflix series,

00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:21.881
Weta was recruited to
build a talking goldfish.

00:07:21.881 --> 00:07:25.454
AJ: Your recovery has been
an inspiration to us all.

00:07:25.454 --> 00:07:27.340
Narrator: This character, AJ Carmichael,

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:28.760
was a new sort of challenge,

00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:31.380
according to animation
supervisor Aidan Martin,

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:33.370
since normal fish don't even have tongues

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:35.360
and there aren't many
parts of a fish's face

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:38.220
that would articulate the
way we see with human speech.

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:40.730
But Weta's animators found a solution:

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:42.860
adding controls to AJ's lips and mouth

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:44.210
so they could overlap,

00:07:44.210 --> 00:07:46.120
allowing a wider range of mouth shapes

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:47.840
as he delivers his lines.

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:49.640
It's all about hitting that sweet spot

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:51.690
between what's anatomically possible

00:07:51.690 --> 00:07:54.090
and what looks credible on screen.

00:07:54.090 --> 00:07:55.530
Weta's mastered that balance

00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:57.270
to create talking animal characters

00:07:57.270 --> 00:07:59.740
that are every bit as
expressive and believable

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:01.620
as their human counterparts.

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:04.770
Caesar: Do not come back.

